WEBVTT

00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:45.345
Planet Express Discount Prisoner Transfer
at your service.

00:00:46.638 --> 00:00:49.057
Take a good look, Dr. Brutaloff.

00:00:49.141 --> 00:00:52.811
This prison is where you'll be spending
your 300-year sentence.

00:00:52.895 --> 00:00:55.856
Yeah, until you're defrosted
and wake up refreshed,

00:00:55.939 --> 00:00:58.066
unaware of any time having passed.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:00.861
He... he heard you!

00:01:00.944 --> 00:01:04.615
Oh, please. It's probably just
the carbonite thawing slightly.

00:01:04.698 --> 00:01:07.993
Take him down to processing
while I stay here and get hooted at.

00:01:11.747 --> 00:01:13.540
Ooh, yeah, baby!

00:01:13.624 --> 00:01:14.917
Oh, stop.

00:01:20.756 --> 00:01:24.843
Fry, look! That's Silicon Red,
the universe's greatest folk singer!

00:01:24.927 --> 00:01:28.972
There's your wallet, your guitar,
and your gun.

00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:30.057
You're free to go.

00:01:30.682 --> 00:01:32.726
Ah, still cocked.

00:01:32.809 --> 00:01:34.811
So, he's a folk singer. So what?

00:01:34.895 --> 00:01:39.191
So what? Have you forgotten
my lifelong dream of being a folk singer?

00:01:39.274 --> 00:01:41.068
Because I sure have until right now.

00:01:41.151 --> 00:01:42.903
Silicon Red! Silicon Red!

00:01:44.154 --> 00:01:46.406
Hey, I have a lifelong dream too.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.159
Of not being left alone
with a frozen supervillain.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:52.037
Don't try anything,
Dr. Brutaloff!

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.247
I know karate.

00:01:58.001 --> 00:02:02.214
Then I shot him and stabbed him
and stole all his gold

00:02:02.297 --> 00:02:06.635
And I tried on his face
while his body turned cold...

00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:08.178
Lovely.

00:02:08.262 --> 00:02:10.222
Just lovely.

00:02:10.305 --> 00:02:12.140
Can I have your autograph,

00:02:12.224 --> 00:02:14.476
and also you tell me everything
you know about folk singing,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.186
and I'll take your guitar too?

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.815
Old Salmonella?
Son, I ought to kill you for asking.

00:02:19.898 --> 00:02:22.734
We met doing hard time
on a Mississippi chain gang.

00:02:22.818 --> 00:02:25.237
And she been with me
through 30-odd convictions.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.448
Thirty? You must be
quite a bungling lowlife.

00:02:28.532 --> 00:02:29.950
Anyhow, about the guitar.

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:36.039
Aw, come on! I can't be a famous
folk singer without a cool guitar.

00:02:36.123 --> 00:02:37.791
Can I at least get a photo?

00:02:37.874 --> 00:02:39.126
And before you answer that...

00:02:40.836 --> 00:02:44.464
Now I'll just have someone
duplicate your guitar. Thanks, sucker.

00:02:44.548 --> 00:02:47.301
You're the sucker, sucker.

00:02:47.384 --> 00:02:50.929
See, with all Salmonella and me
been through, her sound is unique.

00:02:51.013 --> 00:02:54.933
All your fancy technology
will never be able to copy this guitar.

00:02:55.017 --> 00:02:59.563
Using my fancy technology,
I can make an exact copy of this guitar.

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:02.274
Tell me, Dr. Beeler,
will I need to threaten you?

00:03:02.357 --> 00:03:05.986
Not at all. You see, nowadays,
we can take a unique and beautiful object,

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:09.281
and easily reduce it
to a formula for mass production.

00:03:09.364 --> 00:03:11.617
I call the process "science."

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.579
I'll just need to locate that guitar image
on your file system.

00:03:23.795 --> 00:03:25.964
Whoa, that's a lot of porn.

00:03:26.048 --> 00:03:27.799
Where'd you store the guitar picture?

00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:30.135
I don't know. I mainly hang out in here.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.267
Ah-ha, your folk singing folder.

00:03:37.351 --> 00:03:39.228
I'll just double-kick on that.

00:03:39.311 --> 00:03:41.980
Got it.

00:03:42.064 --> 00:03:45.108
Now I'll convert that guitar image
to a wire-frame model.

00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:47.486
There, like so.

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:50.197
And send it wirelessly to my 3D printer.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.212
By laying down layer after layer
of nano-plastic,

00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:08.674
it can turn your wildest dreams
into ordinary reality.

00:04:08.757 --> 00:04:10.801
Witchcraft! Sorcerer!

00:04:11.093 --> 00:04:12.094
Neat.

00:04:13.262 --> 00:04:15.889
- So, how long will it take?
- Four or five hours.

00:04:15.973 --> 00:04:18.809
Shall we adjourn to the porn folder?

00:04:25.107 --> 00:04:27.818
I call my new guitar Bender Mae

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:31.196
because Bender may be
the greatest folk singer of all time!

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.739
It looks pretty authentic.

00:04:32.823 --> 00:04:35.284
You bet it's authentic. See this scratch?

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:37.411
I tell people she got it
while I was doing hard time

00:04:37.494 --> 00:04:39.079
with a New Hampshire chain gang.

00:04:39.162 --> 00:04:40.539
I have a scratch too.

00:04:40.622 --> 00:04:43.458
From where Dr. Brutaloff slashed me
with his finger knives,

00:04:43.542 --> 00:04:45.502
right after he thawed out
of his carbonite,

00:04:45.586 --> 00:04:48.088
and right before he froze me
in his carbonite

00:04:48.171 --> 00:04:50.841
because you deserted me.

00:04:55.095 --> 00:04:57.347
Eh, you're always
getting frozen in stuff.

00:04:57.431 --> 00:04:58.432
It's your thing, man.

00:04:58.515 --> 00:05:01.685
Bender, you're not a folk singer
just 'cause you have a guitar.

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.478
And a flannel shirt.

00:05:03.562 --> 00:05:06.064
I claim I won it
in a knife fight at J. Crew.

00:05:06.148 --> 00:05:08.817
It must be interesting
to win a knife fight.

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:10.319
I wouldn't know.

00:05:10.402 --> 00:05:13.697
The point is,
do you even know any actual folk songs?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.700
Actual folk songs
are all public domain.

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:17.993
Where's the money in that?

00:05:18.076 --> 00:05:19.286
I'll just make up my own.

00:05:19.369 --> 00:05:22.039
But, robot,
you can't just make up folk songs

00:05:22.122 --> 00:05:23.874
like you can a medical diploma.

00:05:23.957 --> 00:05:25.751
They have to come from the heart.

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:27.711
Wrong again, Zoidberg.

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:31.089
While you were yakking, I downloaded
every folk song in the universe

00:05:31.173 --> 00:05:32.215
and analyzed them.

00:05:32.299 --> 00:05:33.967
They're only a few basic patterns.

00:05:34.968 --> 00:05:35.969
For example...

00:05:36.053 --> 00:05:39.389
36% of all folk-song heroes
work on a railroad

00:05:39.473 --> 00:05:40.974
and are named "Big" something.

00:05:41.058 --> 00:05:42.351
Next slide, please.

00:05:43.727 --> 00:05:47.189
75% of these big railroad men
have bad-hearted women

00:05:47.272 --> 00:05:48.315
who done them wrong,

00:05:48.398 --> 00:05:50.484
usually with a smooth-talking rambler.

00:05:51.485 --> 00:05:55.280
There's a lot more, but in the end,
somebody kills somebody, blah, blah, blah.

00:05:55.364 --> 00:05:59.243
Somebody kills somebody
Blah, blah, blah

00:05:59.326 --> 00:06:01.161
- Catchy.
- Not what I meant,

00:06:01.245 --> 00:06:03.038
but don't perform it
without my permission!

00:06:03.121 --> 00:06:05.082
Anyway, using this simple formula,

00:06:05.165 --> 00:06:07.876
I can generate
as many hit folk songs as I want.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.378
All I need is an audience.

00:06:09.461 --> 00:06:12.172
And that's where Silicon Red comes in.

00:06:18.804 --> 00:06:24.226
Man, all this corncob pipe smoke
is irritating my stab wounds.

00:06:24.309 --> 00:06:27.521
Ladies and gentlemen,
I'm proud to present,

00:06:27.604 --> 00:06:31.066
fresh from another down-home
authentic prison term,

00:06:31.149 --> 00:06:33.860
the universe's greatest folk singer,

00:06:33.944 --> 00:06:35.946
Silicon Red!

00:06:38.198 --> 00:06:39.199
Thank you.

00:06:42.119 --> 00:06:43.996
Silicon Red, everyone.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.541
Hey!

00:06:48.208 --> 00:06:51.753
I'm Ramblin' Rodriguez,
the new greatest folk singer ever.

00:06:51.837 --> 00:06:53.922
And I'm gonna sing my new masterpiece,

00:06:54.006 --> 00:06:57.217
The Ballad of Me, Ramblin' Rodriguez.

00:06:57.926 --> 00:06:59.595
Well...

00:06:59.678 --> 00:07:02.556
My bad-hearted woman
loved a smooth-talking gambler

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:05.225
So I ran him over with my train

00:07:05.309 --> 00:07:09.187
Lord, Lord
Yes,I ran him over with my train

00:07:12.941 --> 00:07:15.277
That name again is Ramblin' Rodriguez.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:22.409
Why are they booing me, Silicon?

00:07:22.492 --> 00:07:24.620
'Cause your song was insincere, boy.

00:07:24.703 --> 00:07:26.496
Now, get out and don't come back

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.290
till you've lived a life
worth singing about.

00:07:29.082 --> 00:07:30.083
Yes, sir.

00:07:39.676 --> 00:07:42.679
And don't go to prison. That's my shtick!

00:07:48.602 --> 00:07:51.605
I failed at my lifelong dream again.

00:07:51.688 --> 00:07:55.776
How can I be so bad at everything I try
and still be so great?

00:07:55.859 --> 00:07:58.487
Bender, you have a lovely baritone,

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.281
and you sure can strum
that plastic guitar,

00:08:01.365 --> 00:08:05.202
but you can't write a real folk song
about experiences you haven't had.

00:08:05.285 --> 00:08:07.788
Yeah, you should write a song
about a heartless robot

00:08:07.871 --> 00:08:09.915
who leaves his best friend to be murdered.

00:08:09.998 --> 00:08:12.751
Look, Leela, I'd love to write
about my own experiences...

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:15.963
The heartless robot is Bender.
The best friend is me.

00:08:16.046 --> 00:08:18.215
How can you people be so blind?

00:08:18.298 --> 00:08:21.468
...but I've never picked cotton
or been on top of Old Smokey

00:08:21.552 --> 00:08:23.679
or worked on anything
all the livelong day,

00:08:23.762 --> 00:08:25.138
let alone a railroad.

00:08:25.222 --> 00:08:27.766
Then go live among
the railroad men, why not?

00:08:27.849 --> 00:08:29.518
You'll come to understand them,

00:08:29.601 --> 00:08:32.145
just as I now understand
the ape-men of Earth.

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:36.191
As stupid as Zoidberg's idea is,
it's brilliant when I have it.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.319
I'm off to the railroad
to become a folk singer.

00:08:58.714 --> 00:09:01.675
Uh, I'm looking for
the wrong side of the tracks.

00:09:01.758 --> 00:09:04.595
Then you've come
to the right side of the tracks.

00:09:04.678 --> 00:09:06.013
Have a seat, stranger.

00:09:06.096 --> 00:09:07.931
My name's Bender Rodriguez,

00:09:08.015 --> 00:09:10.267
and I want to learn
the railroading business.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:15.314
Big Caboose. I'm a steel-driving man
working the trans-universal line.

00:09:15.397 --> 00:09:19.359
Wow, you've had the exact experiences
I want to steal for my song.

00:09:19.443 --> 00:09:21.403
Aw, heck, I'm nothing special.

00:09:21.486 --> 00:09:24.531
Just a down-home robot
with a big old hammer.

00:09:24.615 --> 00:09:26.742
A hammer, that should be easy to rhyme.

00:09:26.825 --> 00:09:29.161
- You done time in the slammer?
- Uh, nope.

00:09:29.244 --> 00:09:31.121
- Been to Alabama?
- Sorry.

00:09:31.204 --> 00:09:32.205
How's your grammar?

00:09:32.289 --> 00:09:33.415
Praiseworthy.

00:09:42.799 --> 00:09:45.510
Bender, meet Fast Frank Brogan.

00:09:45.594 --> 00:09:48.680
Daredevil engineer
dies in the crash of Old '88.

00:09:48.764 --> 00:09:50.390
Dandy Jim and Gus.

00:09:50.474 --> 00:09:52.684
Gentlemen hobos with a story to tell.

00:09:52.768 --> 00:09:55.437
I was got drunk and ate my big toe.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:56.563
Can't say I miss it.

00:09:56.647 --> 00:09:58.148
And good old Cookie,

00:09:58.232 --> 00:10:01.401
the crusty comically inept
geological surveyor.

00:10:01.485 --> 00:10:04.029
Jiggers! I dropped my sexton in the stew.

00:10:06.657 --> 00:10:07.950
Been up north

00:10:09.159 --> 00:10:11.119
Been down south

00:10:11.203 --> 00:10:13.413
Got a real weak back

00:10:13.497 --> 00:10:15.874
I got a real big mouth

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:24.049
So while Bender goofs off
at some railroad camp,

00:10:24.132 --> 00:10:27.302
I have to deliver dynamite
to a railroad camp myself.

00:10:27.386 --> 00:10:29.054
And it's the same railroad camp.

00:10:29.137 --> 00:10:31.265
I mean, the irony is palpable.

00:10:32.641 --> 00:10:33.642
Oops.

00:10:33.934 --> 00:10:34.935
Whoopsie.

00:10:35.269 --> 00:10:36.270
My bad.

00:10:40.357 --> 00:10:42.067
Dibs on the toes.

00:10:43.527 --> 00:10:45.737
So how's it going
with the real-world experience?

00:10:45.821 --> 00:10:47.990
Have you found a railroad man
to write a song about?

00:10:48.073 --> 00:10:50.701
Yep, my good friend Big Something here.

00:10:50.784 --> 00:10:52.077
Uh, Caboose.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.664
I'm a steel-driving man,
and there ain't much more to say about me.

00:10:55.747 --> 00:10:57.249
Not till now there wasn't.

00:10:57.332 --> 00:11:02.087
Well, Big Caboose
wasa steel-drivin' son of a gun

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:05.132
Till a bad-hearted woman he spied...

00:11:05.215 --> 00:11:08.802
But, Bender,
I never spied no bad-hearted woman.

00:11:08.886 --> 00:11:10.512
In my song you did,

00:11:10.596 --> 00:11:13.473
a temptress named, I don't know, Jezebel.

00:11:13.557 --> 00:11:15.851
Wow, creativity's hard work.

00:11:18.186 --> 00:11:19.187
Ah.

00:11:19.396 --> 00:11:21.189
You know my favorite part of your song?

00:11:21.273 --> 00:11:23.734
The part where it ended.

00:11:23.817 --> 00:11:26.778
Then, settle in,
'cause that's about a half hour from now.

00:11:26.862 --> 00:11:30.949
Well, Jezebel's heart did wander

00:11:31.033 --> 00:11:34.202
When she saw that ramblin' man

00:11:34.286 --> 00:11:37.873
He was tall and dark and shiny

00:11:37.956 --> 00:11:40.876
And a native Mexican

00:11:40.959 --> 00:11:43.879
That sleazy rambler
sounds an awful lot like you, Bender.

00:11:43.962 --> 00:11:45.714
Hey, thanks for noticing.

00:11:45.797 --> 00:11:48.342
Just for that,
I'm gonna slip you into the 57th verse.

00:11:48.425 --> 00:11:51.178
You can be my best friend
who abandons me when I need you.

00:11:51.261 --> 00:11:53.597
Now, that's a verse we can all enjoy.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.559
Well, Rodriguez ran to his best friend

00:11:57.643 --> 00:12:00.562
Said, "Help me or I'll surely be killed"

00:12:01.021 --> 00:12:04.316
Fry laughed and he said,
"You deserted me once

00:12:04.399 --> 00:12:07.486
"Now it's your turn to feel the chill"
Lord, Lord

00:12:07.569 --> 00:12:11.448
"Now it's your turn to feel the chill"

00:12:12.699 --> 00:12:15.911
You've accurately portrayed
the nature of my grievance.

00:12:21.041 --> 00:12:24.628
Big Caboose went lookin' for Rodriguez

00:12:24.711 --> 00:12:27.631
Straight to New New York town

00:12:27.714 --> 00:12:28.715
He cried...

00:12:28.799 --> 00:12:30.217
Bender, guess what.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.554
I met a fabulous girl
and we're engaged to be hitched.

00:12:33.637 --> 00:12:34.888
That's great, Caboose.

00:12:34.972 --> 00:12:36.515
Come on in and set a spell.

00:12:36.598 --> 00:12:37.683
Oh, no time.

00:12:37.766 --> 00:12:39.351
I got someplace to be.

00:12:39.434 --> 00:12:41.019
See you soon, honeybunch.

00:12:41.103 --> 00:12:42.104
Okay, dumpling.

00:12:43.522 --> 00:12:46.066
My man's gone, gone.

00:12:46.483 --> 00:12:49.319
Oh, I'm so lonely and easy.

00:12:49.987 --> 00:12:51.071
Oh.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:03.166
Oh, yeah.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.419
This is definitely going in the song.

00:13:05.711 --> 00:13:09.089
He cried,
"Rambler,you slept with my Jezebel"

00:13:09.172 --> 00:13:11.174
Hey, what's your name, anyhow?

00:13:11.258 --> 00:13:12.259
Jezebel.

00:13:12.342 --> 00:13:14.428
Oh, good,
I won't have to change any words.

00:13:23.103 --> 00:13:25.230
Yeah, you're right. It's getting late.

00:13:25.314 --> 00:13:26.315
I'll get your coat.

00:13:28.442 --> 00:13:29.985
Oh, hey, Big Caboose.

00:13:30.068 --> 00:13:31.528
What are you doing here on Earth?

00:13:31.612 --> 00:13:34.781
I'm here for to see Ramblin' Rodriguez.

00:13:34.865 --> 00:13:36.742
Well, he's at the railroad camp.

00:13:36.825 --> 00:13:41.747
I know, but some strange force
drew me to New New York town.

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:44.875
That rambler slept with my Jezebel

00:13:44.958 --> 00:13:48.795
Now I'm comin' for to shoot him down
Lord, Lord

00:13:48.879 --> 00:13:52.591
Comin' for to shoot him down

00:13:54.009 --> 00:13:56.553
Guess I'll go back
to the railroad camp now.

00:13:57.471 --> 00:14:00.807
That's weird,
he sounded a lot like Bender's folk song.

00:14:00.891 --> 00:14:05.312
Not a lot like Bender's folk song,
exactly like et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:05.395 --> 00:14:07.481
His song is coming true somehow.

00:14:07.564 --> 00:14:11.318
Oh, my God,
reality is infringing Bender's copyright.

00:14:13.487 --> 00:14:15.948
"I'm comin' for to shoot you down"

00:14:16.031 --> 00:14:17.032
No, wait.

00:14:17.115 --> 00:14:19.826
"I'm comin' for to run you down"

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:21.745
With a train. Get it?

00:14:39.429 --> 00:14:42.432
It's over, Jezebel.
Grab your things and get out.

00:14:42.516 --> 00:14:44.059
I didn't bring any things.

00:14:44.142 --> 00:14:46.311
That's what I like best about you, baby.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:55.821
Rambler, you slept with my Jezebel,

00:14:55.904 --> 00:14:58.991
and I'm comin' for to run you down,
Lord, Lord.

00:14:59.074 --> 00:15:03.203
I'm comin' for to run you down

00:15:43.744 --> 00:15:45.954
Fry, help! Let me in.

00:15:47.164 --> 00:15:49.041
Bender?

00:15:49.124 --> 00:15:51.084
What are you doing? It's 3:00 a.m.

00:15:51.168 --> 00:15:52.836
I don't care if it's three B. M.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.338
Open up and hide me!

00:15:54.421 --> 00:15:55.839
I don't think so, Bender.

00:15:55.923 --> 00:15:57.257
You deserted me once,

00:15:57.341 --> 00:16:00.802
and now it's your turn to
"feel the chill."

00:16:00.886 --> 00:16:01.887
Ha!

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:13.232
Breaker, breaker.

00:16:13.315 --> 00:16:14.399
Hola.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.444
Professor, help.

00:16:17.527 --> 00:16:19.905
The hero of my folk song
tried to run me down,

00:16:19.988 --> 00:16:21.865
and Fry refused to save me.

00:16:21.949 --> 00:16:24.451
- That doesn't sound like me.
- Fry?

00:16:24.534 --> 00:16:27.120
But you just...
Eh, I'll figure it out later.

00:16:27.204 --> 00:16:29.289
But first I'll kill you!

00:16:30.332 --> 00:16:31.875
Bender, stop.

00:16:31.959 --> 00:16:33.919
We've been trying to warn you all night.

00:16:34.002 --> 00:16:36.004
You didn't answer
your cell phone telephone,

00:16:36.088 --> 00:16:38.006
so the Professor tried his CB.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:40.425
Uh, scratch that, Rubber Turkey.

00:16:40.509 --> 00:16:42.469
We got a 20 on the tin man.

00:16:42.803 --> 00:16:44.221
Toodle-oo.

00:16:45.305 --> 00:16:47.849
But Fry betrayed me at our apartment!

00:16:47.933 --> 00:16:49.726
Just like in your folk song.

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:53.397
Somehow everything you sang about
is happening in real life.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:55.315
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:16:55.399 --> 00:16:59.361
Are you saying that guy at the apartment
was some kind of exact copy of Fry?

00:16:59.444 --> 00:17:00.445
'Cause that's crazy.

00:17:00.529 --> 00:17:02.614
He's a unique and beautiful object.

00:17:03.782 --> 00:17:04.783
The printer.

00:17:07.244 --> 00:17:10.163
Oh, I must have left
the wireless connection

00:17:10.247 --> 00:17:12.207
to your folk song directory open.

00:17:12.291 --> 00:17:15.836
Everything you wrote in the song
got routed straight to the 3-D printer.

00:17:15.919 --> 00:17:18.880
Big Caboose, duplicate Fry, even Cookie

00:17:18.964 --> 00:17:21.758
the crusty, comically inept
geological surveyor,

00:17:21.842 --> 00:17:23.635
they're all made of nano-plastic.

00:17:23.719 --> 00:17:26.054
So, if Bender had sung about,
I don't know,

00:17:26.138 --> 00:17:28.557
some crazy giant land octopus
attacking me,

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.100
it would have actually happened?

00:17:30.183 --> 00:17:33.645
I can't even imagine
singing about such a thing.

00:17:34.271 --> 00:17:35.814
Well...

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:37.649
Fry, run!

00:17:37.733 --> 00:17:41.778
Aw, relax, Beeler's stupid machine
takes hours to print anything.

00:17:41.862 --> 00:17:42.905
No, it doesn't.

00:17:42.988 --> 00:17:45.991
I improved it
using fancy modern technology.

00:17:54.333 --> 00:17:56.793
Bender, whatever you do,

00:17:56.877 --> 00:18:00.255
don't think about any more octopuses.

00:18:00.339 --> 00:18:02.215
Octopuses?

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:06.011
I believe the correct plural is octopi.

00:18:08.597 --> 00:18:12.100
I'm sorry.
"Octopuses" is also acceptable.

00:18:12.976 --> 00:18:14.770
Think about puppies, Bender,

00:18:14.853 --> 00:18:16.897
cute, harmless, dead puppies.

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:19.441
Don't worry.
I'll disconnect him from the printer.

00:18:19.524 --> 00:18:22.527
No, wait, I know how to save Bender
from Big Caboose.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.532
All he has to do
is write his way out of this mess,

00:18:27.616 --> 00:18:29.785
and the 3-D printer will make it happen.

00:18:29.868 --> 00:18:31.787
- Like how?
- Well, I don't know.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:35.207
Make up another giant octopus
and have it kill Big Caboose.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.084
What kind of lazy ending is that?

00:18:37.167 --> 00:18:39.002
I'm not gonna put that in my song.

00:18:39.086 --> 00:18:40.837
Hey, I have a cool idea.

00:18:40.921 --> 00:18:42.256
Create a duplicate Bender,

00:18:42.339 --> 00:18:45.509
so when Big Caboose kills it,
it's not real, it's duplicate.

00:18:45.592 --> 00:18:48.470
Ooh, ooh, they did that on Star Trek: TNG.

00:18:48.554 --> 00:18:49.638
Cram it, nerd.

00:18:49.721 --> 00:18:53.225
I'm not going to just copy
some feel-good TV show wrap-up.

00:18:54.851 --> 00:18:56.895
I can think of a hundred endings
that'll save you.

00:18:56.979 --> 00:18:58.146
Just pick one.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:01.483
No, Dr. Beeler, I won't just pick one.

00:19:01.567 --> 00:19:05.279
My audience deserves better
than some crappy, formulaic ending.

00:19:05.362 --> 00:19:10.367
If I must die, let me die
in a blaze of artistic integrity.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.869
WELL...

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:24.715
Then the steel-drivin' man

00:19:24.798 --> 00:19:27.009
Rammed his train through the wall

00:19:27.092 --> 00:19:31.597
And crushed him flatter than a MacBook Air
Lord, Lord

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:36.476
Crushed him flatter than a MacBook Air

00:19:37.102 --> 00:19:38.729
But while you were all out fighting

00:19:38.812 --> 00:19:40.856
With the giant plastic octopi

00:19:40.939 --> 00:19:42.482
I copied myself, you see...

00:19:42.566 --> 00:19:43.692
Bender!

00:19:43.775 --> 00:19:45.402
And I cowered in the bathroom

00:19:45.485 --> 00:19:47.154
While that steel-drivin' moron

00:19:47.237 --> 00:19:50.574
-Killed a duplicate instead of me
-Lord, lord

00:19:50.657 --> 00:19:55.120
Killed a duplicate instead of me

00:19:57.998 --> 00:20:00.250
Now, that's what I call a great ending.

00:20:00.334 --> 00:20:03.712
I made a copy of myself
and Big Caboose killed him.

00:20:03.795 --> 00:20:07.132
But you said making a copy of yourself
would be a lousy ending.

00:20:07.215 --> 00:20:09.968
No, duplicate me said that.

00:20:10.052 --> 00:20:12.721
See, I had to be sure he wouldn't try
and weasel out of getting killed

00:20:12.804 --> 00:20:14.431
like I would and did,

00:20:14.514 --> 00:20:18.393
so I created him with a tragic weakness:
artistic integrity.

00:20:18.477 --> 00:20:19.561
Of course!

00:20:19.645 --> 00:20:23.482
How could we have thought a guy
with integrity was you even for a second?

00:20:23.565 --> 00:20:25.484
Because you're idiots.

00:20:25.567 --> 00:20:28.028
Come on, Silicon Red, let's sell out.

00:20:35.285 --> 00:20:37.788
I'm the real Rodriguez,
will you please stand up?

00:20:37.871 --> 00:20:40.123
He been workin' on the railroad
Ladies can't get enough

00:20:40.207 --> 00:20:42.834
I made a 3-D version of my worst nightmare

00:20:42.918 --> 00:20:45.254
Sucker tried to stop the printer
and I found him there

00:20:45.337 --> 00:20:48.048
Went 187 on his robot ass

00:20:48.131 --> 00:20:50.342
Turned out to be a copy of my robot ass

00:20:50.425 --> 00:20:52.761
Now we're folk-singin' players
packin' Beaujolais

00:20:52.844 --> 00:20:55.597
And makin' max contributions to our 401K

00:20:56.306 --> 00:20:57.307
Peace out.